▶️ How to Run

No installation or build step is required.

Download or clone the repository.
Open index.html directly in any modern browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari).
Or open the folder in VS Code and use the Live Server extension for live-reload while editing.
bash
git clone https://github.com/mahi03052003/Smart-Event-Management-Platform
cd aurelia-events
# then open index.html in your browser

To inspect saved data: open DevTools → Application tab → Local Storage, and look for aurelia_registrations and the private-request storage key.

🧩 How It Works — Architecture Overview
Data layer (data.js): a single EVENTS array holds every event's id, title, category, date, location, seats, and desc. Every feature — cards, search, filters, details, registration — reads from this one source.
Rendering (main.js): small, focused functions (cardHTML(), renderGrid(), renderFeatured(), renderTicker(), renderDashboard()) turn data into HTML strings and inject them into the DOM. Adding a new event to data.js requires zero HTML changes.
View routing: the homepage, event details, and dashboard are separate <section>s toggled with a .hidden class rather than separate pages — kept simple with plain JavaScript, no router library.
Search & filtering: applyFilters() narrows EVENTS by the active category chip, then further narrows by the search input, combining both conditions with AND logic.
Forms & validation: both the registration form and the private-event enquiry form validate on submit using regex checks for email/phone and required-field checks, toggling an .error class per field with inline messages.
Persistence: localStorage (via small get/save wrapper functions) stores registrations and private-event requests as JSON, keyed by a generated reference code, so data survives page reloads without a backend.
Feedback: successful actions swap a form view for a confirmation view (with a reference code) rather than using intrusive browser alerts; cancellations show a toast notification.

Registration and enquiry modals scroll internally on short screens (max-height: 88vh) so they never overflow the viewport.

Deployed as a static site via GitHub Pages.

